comments.message:
IIUC, I have to do it like this:
```
    for (const auto& [boardPath, paths] : probePaths)
    {
        for (const auto& probePath : paths)
        {
            result[boardPath].emplace_back("probed_by", "probing", probePath);
            result[probePath].emplace_back("probing", "probed_by", boardPath);
        }
    }
```
Now I basically have a bidirectional association between boardPath and each probePath. 

Did you mean it like that?

Correction:

```
for (const auto& [boardPath, paths] : probePaths)
    {
        for (const auto& probePath : paths)
        {
            result[boardPath].emplace_back("probed_by", "probing", probePath);
        }
    }
```

In the .hpp you have
```
    // Represents the mapping between inventory object pathes of a
    // probed configuration and the object paths of DBus interfaces
    // it was probed on.
    std::unordered_map<Path, std::set<Path>> probePaths;
```

So i would assume the key is always the probed element and the value is the object paths it was probed on.

That also lines up with your changes
```
for (const auto& [boardPath, paths] : probePaths)
    {
        for (const auto& probePath : paths)
        {
            result[boardPath].emplace_back("probed_by", "probing", probePath);
        }
    }
```

where `boardPath` is mentioned as variable name.

But here on line 155 you insert `probePath` as a key into this map, which does not look right to me.


```suggestion
    probePaths[boardPath].emplace(probePath);
```
